<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="appendix-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vectors</title>

  <subsection>
    <title>Dot Products</title>
    <p>The <term>dot product</term> is a way to measure angles between vectors.  
    Let <m>\vec{u} = \langle u_1,u_2\rangle</m> and 
    <m>\vec{v} = \langle v_1, v_2\rangle</m> be vectors in <m>\R^2</m>.
    </p> 
    <figure xml:id="fig-dot-product">
      <image width="50%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dot-product">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/dot-product.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <caption>The angle <m>\theta</m> between the vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>.</caption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Then by the law of cosines, we have
      <md>|\vec{u}-\vec{v}|^2  = |\vec{u}^2| + |\vec{v}^2|-2|\vec{u}||\vec{v}|\cos(\theta) </md>.
      Noting that <m>\vec{u}-\vec{v}=\langle u_1-v_1, u_2-v_2 \rangle</m>,
      we have
      <md>(u_1-v_1)^2+(u_2-v_2)^2  = (u_1^2+u_2^2) + (v_1^2+v_2^2)-2|\vec{u}||\vec{v}|\cos(\theta) </md>.
      Then
      <md>
        <mrow>|\vec{u}||\vec{v}|\cos(\theta) \amp = \frac{1}{2}\left((u_1^2+u_2^2) + (v_1^2+v_2^2)-(u_1-v_1)^2-(u_2-v_2)^2\right) </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = u_1v_1+u_2v_2</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-dot-product">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vec{u},\vec{v} \in \R^n</m> with 
          <m>\vec{u}=\langle u_1,\ldots,u_n\rangle</m> and
          <m>\vec{v}=\langle v_1,\ldots,v_n\rangle</m>.
          Then the <term>dot product</term> of <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> is
          <md>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = u_1v_1+\cdots+u_nv_n</md>.
        </p>
        <p>Note that the dot product of two vectors produces a <em>scalar</em>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <observation>
      <p>The angle <m>\theta</m> between the vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>
        is given by
        <md>\cos(\theta) = \frac{\vec{u}\cdot\vec{v}}{|\vec{u}||\vec{v}|}</md>.
      </p>
    </observation>

    <p>An immediate consequence of this is:</p>
    <fact xml:id="fact-dot-product-orthogonal">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>\vec{u},\vec{v} \in \R^n</m>.  Then <m>\vec{u}</m> and 
          <m>\vec{v}</m> are orthogonal if and only if <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> are orthogonal precisely when
          <m>\cos(\theta)=0</m>, which happens if and only if <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}=0</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <p>Because of its simple algebraic definition, it is straightforward to see
    that the dot product has a number of useful properties.</p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-dot-product-properties">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>\vec{u},\vec{v},\vec{w} \in \R^n</m> and <m>c \in \R</m>. Then
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = \vec{v} \cdot \vec{u}</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>\left(\vec{u}+\vec{v}\right) \cdot \vec{w} = \vec{u} \cdot \vec{w}+\vec{v}\cdot\vec{w}</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>\left(c \vec{u}\right) \cdot \vec{v} = c \vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}</m>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </fact>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Cross Products</title>
    <p>Given two vectors <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in \R^3</m>, we often need 
      to find a third vector <m>\vec{w}</m> that is orthogonal to both
      <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>; equivalently, <m>\vec{w}</m>
      should be a vector that is orthogonal (or normal) to the plane
      spanned by <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>. We will use <m>\times</m>
      to represent this operation, and investigate properties we desire it
      to have in order to define it.
    </p>

    <activity>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\hat{i}</m> and <m>\hat{j}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\hat{j}</m> and <m>\hat{k}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\hat{i}</m> and <m>\hat{k}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\hat{i}</m> and <m>\hat{i}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </activity>

    <remark>
      <p>Since there is an entire plane's worth of vectors orthogonal to a single
      vector and no good way to choose among them, we will insist <m>\vec{v} \times \vec{v}=\vec{0}</m>
      for any vector <m>\vec{v}</m>. Similarly, we will have <m>\vec{0} \times \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>To make a definition of <m>\times</m>, we will have to make some choices.
      The usual convention is to have a <term>right-handed coordinate system</term>
      and set
      <md>
        <mrow> \hat{i} \times \hat{j} \amp =  \hat{k} \amp
               \hat{j} \times \hat{k} \amp = \hat{i} \amp
               \hat{k} \times \hat{i} \amp =  \hat{j}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>We would like our operation <m>\times</m> to distribute, in which case
      we have 
      <md>\vec{0}=(\hat{i} + \hat{j}) \times (\hat{i} + \hat{j}) = 
        \hat{i} \times \hat{i} + \hat{i} \times \hat{j}+ \hat{j}\times\hat{i} + \hat{j} \times \hat{j}
        =\hat{i} \times \hat{j} + \hat{j}\times\hat{i}
      </md>
      Thus we have 
      <md>
        <mrow> \hat{j} \times \hat{i} \amp = -\hat{k} \amp
               \hat{k} \times \hat{j} \amp = -\hat{i} \amp
               \hat{i} \times \hat{k} \amp = -\hat{j}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now, let <m>\vec{u}=u_1 \hat{i}+u_2\hat{j}+u_3\hat{k}</m> and
      <m>\vec{v}=v_1\hat{i}+v_2\hat{j}+v_3\hat{k}</m>.  Then we would have
      <md>
        <mrow> 
          \vec{u} \times \vec{v} 
          \amp = \left(u_1 \hat{i}+u_2\hat{j}+u_3\hat{k}\right) \times 
                 \left(v_1\hat{i}+v_2\hat{j}+v_3\hat{k}\right)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = u_1 v_1 \hat{i} \times \hat{i} + u_1 v_2 \hat{i} \times \hat{j} + u_1 v_3 \hat{i} \times \hat{k} +
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \phantom{=} u_2 v_1 \hat{j} \times \hat{i} + u_2 v_2 \hat{j} \times \hat{j} + u_2 v_3 \hat{j} \times \hat{k} +
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \phantom{=} u_3 v_1 \hat{k} \times \hat{i} + u_3 v_2 \hat{k} \times \hat{j} + u_3 v_3 \hat{k} \times \hat{k} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = (u_1v_2-u_2v_1) \hat{i} \times \hat{j} + (u_2v_3-v_3u_2) \hat{j} \times \hat{k} + (u_1v_3-u_3v_1) \hat{i} \times \hat{k}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp =  (u_2v_3-v_3u_2) \hat{i} - (u_1v_3-u_3v_1) \hat{j}+ (u_1v_2-u_2v_1)\hat{k}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-cross-product">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vec{u}=u_1 \hat{i}+u_2\hat{j}+u_3\hat{k}</m> and
          <m>\vec{v}=v_1\hat{i}+v_2\hat{j}+v_3\hat{k}</m>. We define the
          <term>cross product</term> as
          <md>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} = (u_2v_3-v_3u_2) \hat{i} - (u_1v_3-u_3v_1) \hat{j}+ (u_1v_2-u_2v_1)\hat{k} </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>It is straightforward to check the following.</p>
    <observation>
      <p>Let <m>\vec{u},\vec{v} \in \R^3</m>.  Then
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{u} \cdot (\vec{u} \times \vec{v}) = 0</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{v} \cdot (\vec{u} \times \vec{v}) = 0</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </observation>
  </subsection>


</section>